The optimal strategy for sorting the cards would be to sort them one at a time, flipping each card over to check its color and placing it in the appropriate pile based on its number and color. This strategy ensures that each card is sorted correctly and efficiently without the need for any additional sorting algorithms.
Here is the Swift code to implement this strategy:
```
// Create empty arrays for each pile
var oddNumbers: [Int] = []
var evenNumbers: [Int] = []
var negativeNumbers: [Int] = []
// Loop through each card
for card in cards {
    // Check the color of the card
    if card.color == "red" {
        // Check the number of the card and add it to the appropriate pile
        if card.number % 2 == 1 {
            oddNumbers.append(card.number)
        } else {
            evenNumbers.append(card.number)
        }
    } else {
        // Check the number of the card and add it to the negative numbers pile
        if card.number < 0 {
            negativeNumbers.append(card.number)
        }
    }
}
// Print out the sorted piles
print("Odd Numbers: \(oddNumbers)")
print("Even Numbers: \(evenNumbers)")
print("Negative Numbers: \(negativeNumbers)")
```
Note: This code assumes that the `cards` array contains objects with `number` and `color` properties. You may need to adjust the code to fit your specific implementation.

