Here is a C code segment that takes two numbers as inputs and returns their product with the consideration of overflow cases and the performance optimization in terms of time and space complexity:
```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <limits.h>
int safe_multiply(int a, int b) {
    if (a == 0 || b == 0) {
        return 0;
    }
    if (a == INT_MIN && b == -1 || b == INT_MIN && a == -1) {
        return INT_MAX;
    }
    int result = a * b;
    if (a == result / b) {
        return result;
    }
    return INT_MAX;
}
int main() {
    int a, b, result;
    printf("Enter two numbers: ");
    scanf("%d %d", &a, &b);
    result = safe_multiply(a, b);
    printf("The product is: %d\n", result);
    return 0;
}
```
This code segment uses the following approach to handle overflow cases:
- If either of the input numbers is 0, the result is 0.
- If either of the input numbers is INT_MIN and the other number is -1, the result is INT_MAX.
- Otherwise, the product of the two numbers is calculated and checked for overflow by dividing the result by one of the input numbers. If the quotient is equal to the other input number, the result is safe and returned. Otherwise, the result is INT_MAX.
This approach has a time complexity of O(1) and a space complexity of O(1), making it highly optimized for performance.

Here's an example Java code snippet that demonstrates the usage of the passive voice in a sentence:
```java
public static String convertToPassiveVoice(String sentence) throws IllegalArgumentException {
    String[] words = sentence.split(" ");
    String object = "";
    int verbIndex = -1;
    for (int i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
        if (words[i].endsWith("ing")) {
            verbIndex = i;
        }
        if (words[i].equals("the")) {
            object = words[i+1];
        }
    }
    if (verbIndex == -1 || object.equals("")) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input is not a valid sentence in active voice.");
    }
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    sb.append(object).append(" is being ").append(words[verbIndex]);
    for (int i = verbIndex + 1; i < words.length; i++) {
        sb.append(" ").append(words[i]);
    }
    return sb.toString();
}
```
This method takes a string input containing an active voice sentence and returns the same sentence in passive voice. It first splits the sentence into an array of words and then looks for the verb that ends with "ing" and the object of the sentence. If either of these is not found, it throws an `IllegalArgumentException`.
If the input is valid, it constructs a new sentence in passive voice by appending the object, "is being", and the verb, and then appending the rest of the words in the original sentence. Finally, it returns the new sentence as a string.
Note that this code snippet is just an example and may not work for all cases of active voice sentences. It also assumes that the object of the sentence comes immediately after the word "the".

