    headerRow.createCell(1).setCellValue("Event")
    headerRow.createCell(2).setCellValue("Notable Figure")
    headerRow.createCell(3).setCellValue("Cultural Landmark")
    // Loop through each table row and extract data
    var rowIndex = 1
    for (table <- tables) {
      val rows = table.select("tr")
      for (row <- rows) {
        val cells = row.select("td")
        if (cells.size() == 3) {
          val year = cells.get(0).text()
          val event = cells.get(1).text()
          val figure = cells.get(2).select("a").text()
          val landmark = cells.get(2).select("i").text()
          // Create new row in Excel sheet and add data
          val dataRow = sheet.createRow(rowIndex)
          dataRow.createCell(0).setCellValue(year)
          dataRow.createCell(1).setCellValue(event)
          dataRow.createCell(2).setCellValue(figure)
          dataRow.createCell(3).setCellValue(landmark)
          rowIndex += 1
        }
      }
    }
    // Auto-size columns and save Excel file
    for (i <- 0 until 4) {
      sheet.autoSizeColumn(i)
    }
    val fileOut = new FileOutputStream(new File("TokyoHistory.xlsx"))
    workbook.write(fileOut)
    fileOut.close()
    workbook.close()
    println("Tokyo history data has been scraped and saved to an Excel file.")
  }
}
```
This code connects to the History of Tokyo Wikipedia page, selects all tables with the "wikitable" class, and loops through each table row to extract the year, event, notable figure, and cultural landmark data. It then creates a new row in an Excel sheet and adds the data to separate columns for each category. Finally, it auto-sizes the columns and saves the Excel file to the current directory. 
Note that this code assumes that the Wikipedia page structure and data format will remain consistent over time. If the page structure or data format changes, the code may need to be updated accordingly.

