How can I implement a more efficient and scalable function in C++ that checks if a given string contains a specific substring with complex edge cases? For example, what if the input string is very large and cannot be stored in memory, or if the substring needs to be matched with case sensitivity? Can you provide an optimized code snippet for this function that handles these cases? Additionally, how can we ensure the function is thread-safe and can handle concurrent access? 
[C++ code]
```cpp
bool containsSubstring(const std::string& str, const std::string& substr, bool caseSensitive = true) {
  if (str.empty() || substr.empty()) {
    return false;
  }
  if (!caseSensitive) {
    auto it = std::search(str.begin(), str.end(), substr.begin(), substr.end(),
      [](char ch1, char ch2) { return std::toupper(ch1) == std::toupper(ch2); });
    return it != str.end();
  }
  else {
    return str.find(substr) != std::string::npos;
  }
}
```
This function takes two string inputs, `str` and `substr`, and returns a boolean value indicating whether `str` contains `substr`. It also includes an optional parameter for case sensitivity. The function checks if either input is empty and returns `false` if that is the case. It then uses the `find` method of the `string` class to search for `substr` in `str` if case sensitivity is enabled. If case sensitivity is disabled, it uses the `search` function from the `<algorithm>` library to search for `substr` in `str` using case-insensitive matching. To handle edge cases such as large input strings, the function uses `const` references for the input strings to avoid making unnecessary copies. To handle concurrency, additional synchronization mechanisms such as locks or atomic variables may need to be added to ensure thread safety.

output:
To handle large input strings that cannot be stored in memory, we can use a streaming approach where we read the input string and substring in chunks and process them iteratively. This approach avoids loading the entire string into memory at once, which can be more memory-efficient and faster.
Here's an optimized code snippet that implements this approach:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cctype>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
bool containsSubstring(const std::string& str, const std::string& substr, bool caseSensitive = true) {
  if (str.empty() || substr.empty()) {
    return false;
  }
  if (!caseSensitive) {
    auto it = std::search(str.begin(), str.end(), substr.begin(), substr.end(),
      [](char ch1, char ch2) { return std::toupper(ch1) == std::toupper(ch2); });
    return it != str.end();
  }
  else {
    return str.find(substr) != std::string::npos;
  }
}
bool containsSubstringStream(const std::string& filename, const std::string& substr, bool caseSensitive = true, int chunkSize = 1024) {
  std::ifstream file(filename);
  if (!file.is_open()) {
    return false;
  }
  std::string buffer(chunkSize, '\0');
  std::string leftover;
  std::mutex mutex;
  bool found = false;
  while (!found && file) {
    file.read(&buffer[0], chunkSize);
    std::streamsize bytesRead = file.gcount();
    buffer.resize(bytesRead);
    buffer = leftover + buffer;
    leftover.clear();
    if (bytesRead < chunkSize) {
      file.clear();
    }
    auto it = std::search(buffer.begin(), buffer.end(), substr.begin(), substr.end(),
      [caseSensitive](char ch1, char ch2) { return caseSensitive ? ch1 == ch2 : std::toupper(ch1) == std::toupper(ch2); });
    if (it != buffer.end()) {
      found = true;
    }
    else if (bytesRead == chunkSize) {
      int lastMatchIndex = buffer.size() - substr.size();
      if (lastMatchIndex > 0) {
        leftover = buffer.substr(lastMatchIndex);
        buffer.resize(lastMatchIndex);
      }
    }
  }
  file.close();
  return found;
}
```
This function, `containsSubstringStream`, takes a filename instead of a string and reads the file in chunks of size `chunkSize` (default 1024 bytes) using an `ifstream`. It also includes a `mutex` object to ensure thread safety in case the function is called concurrently. The `leftover` string is used to store the end of the previous chunk that may contain a partial match of the substring. The function returns `true` if the substring is found in the file and `false` otherwise.
To ensure thread safety, we can use a `std::lock_guard` to lock the `mutex` object before accessing the shared resources:
```cpp
bool containsSubstringStreamThreadSafe(const std::string& filename, const std::string& substr, bool caseSensitive = true, int chunkSize = 1024) {
  std::ifstream file(filename);
  if (!file.is_open()) {
    return false;
  }
  std::string buffer(chunkSize, '\0');
  std::string leftover;
  std::mutex mutex;
  bool found = false;
  while (!found && file) {
    file.read(&buffer[0], chunkSize);
    std::streamsize bytesRead = file.gcount();
    buffer.resize(bytesRead);
    buffer = leftover + buffer;
    leftover.clear();
    if (bytesRead < chunkSize) {
      file.clear();
    }
    {
      std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
      auto it = std::search(buffer.begin(), buffer.end(), substr.begin(), substr.end(),
        [caseSensitive](char ch1, char ch2) { return caseSensitive ? ch1 == ch2 : std::toupper(ch1) == std::toupper(ch2); });
      if (it != buffer.end()) {
        found = true;
      }
      else if (bytesRead == chunkSize) {
        int lastMatchIndex = buffer.size() - substr.size();
        if (lastMatchIndex > 0) {
          leftover = buffer.substr(lastMatchIndex);
          buffer.resize(lastMatchIndex);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  file.close();
  return found;
}
```
In this version of the function, we use a `std::lock_guard` to lock the `mutex` object before accessing the shared resources inside the `while` loop. This ensures that only one thread can access the shared resources at a time and prevents data races.

