The most efficient algorithm for computing the GCD of two large integers is the Binary GCD algorithm, also known as Stein's algorithm. It is based on the observation that if both numbers are even, then their GCD is also even, and if one number is even and the other is odd, then their GCD is equal to the GCD of the odd number and the even number divided by 2. This reduces the problem to computing the GCD of two smaller numbers, which can be done recursively until both numbers become odd.
Here's a sample implementation of the Binary GCD algorithm in PHP:
```
function gcd($a, $b) {
    if ($a == 0) {
        return $b;
    }
    if ($b == 0) {
        return $a;
    }
    $shift = 0;
    while (($a & 1) == 0 && ($b & 1) == 0) {
        $a >>= 1;
        $b >>= 1;
        $shift++;
    }
    while (($a & 1) == 0) {
        $a >>= 1;
    }
    do {
        while (($b & 1) == 0) {
            $b >>= 1;
        }
        if ($a > $b) {
            $temp = $a;
            $a = $b;
            $b = $temp;
        }
        $b -= $a;
    } while ($b != 0);
    return $a << $shift;
}
```
This implementation uses bitwise operations to perform the necessary arithmetic operations, which are much faster than using regular arithmetic operators. The algorithm has a time complexity of O(log n), where n is the maximum of the two input numbers, and a space complexity of O(1), since it only requires a constant amount of memory to store the intermediate values.
