To handle multiple requests simultaneously using concurrency, you can use Goroutines and channels. Here is an example:
```go
type AnagramResult struct {
    String1     string
    String2     string
    AreAnagrams bool
}
func checkAnagrams(stringsChan chan [2]string, resultsChan chan AnagramResult) {
    for strings := range stringsChan {
        areAnagrams := areAnagrams(strings[0], strings[1])
        result := AnagramResult{strings[0], strings[1], areAnagrams}
        resultsChan <- result
    }
}
func main() {
    stringsChan := make(chan [2]string)
    resultsChan := make(chan AnagramResult)
    for i := 0; i < 10; i++ {
        go checkAnagrams(stringsChan, resultsChan)
    }
    for i := 0; i < 100; i++ {
        strings := [2]string{"hello", "world"}
        stringsChan <- strings
    }
    close(stringsChan)
    for i := 0; i < 100; i++ {
        result := <-resultsChan
        fmt.Printf("%s and %s are anagrams: %v\n", result.String1, result.String2, result.AreAnagrams)
    }
}
```
This code creates two channels: `stringsChan` to receive pairs of strings to check, and `resultsChan` to send the results. It then starts 10 Goroutines to handle the requests concurrently. Finally, it sends 100 requests to `stringsChan`, waits for the results, and prints them.

